I find things like !rn2(...), rather than rn2(...) !=3D 0, very
confusing myself; they've been forbidden in all coding
guidelines I've had a hand in.  (Basically, the comparison
operators are considered to return bool, and conditions, !, &&,
|| and the first operand of ?: are required to take a bool.)

> [One of the reasons for this, in turn, is that
> "standard" C coding style doesn't stack braces,
> but puts the opening brace at the end of the opening
> line making deep nesting very hard to understand,
> and so shortcuts are used to avoid deep nesting.]

> Written as I prefer to see structured code written, so the
> logic and nesting levels of boolean operators are easily
> identified, the affirmative version of just the above code
> (the original function is more complex than this) would read:

>    if
>    (
>      (
>        (
>          depth( &u.uz )
>          >
>          0
>        )
>        &&
>        (
>          (
>            rn2( 1 + ( ( depth( &u.uz ) / 4 ) ) )
>          )
>          >
>          0
>        )
>      )
>    )
>    {
>      return /* some ok_to_drop_bones result */;
>    }

Well, you don't have to exagerate quite that much, but line
breaks and nesting should respect operator precedence;
personally, when chaning expressions, I'll also put the && and
|| at the start of the line, so that the chaining is readily
visible.

